{"id":"persona-5s4b.61","title":"e2e test synchronises on a 100ms sleep and leaks its socket on failure","description":"persona-grpc/tests/e2e.rs:98-99:

    // Give the server a moment to bind.
    sleep(Duration::from_millis(100)).await;

What goes wrong: the test's correctness depends on an unwritten timing assumption — that serve() finishes create_dir_all + bind + set_permissions (persona-grpc/src/server.rs:18-33) within 100ms. On a loaded CI runner or under a debug-build cold start that is not guaranteed, and the failure surfaces as 'client connect failed' at e2e.rs:113, which points at the client rather than at the race. There is no retry, so it is a straight flake.

The readiness condition is observable rather than guessable: the socket file's existence is exactly what the client needs. Rewrite as a bounded poll:

    for _ in 0..100 {
        if std::path::Path::new(&socket_path).exists() { break; }
        sleep(Duration::from_millis(10)).await;
    }

That is faster in the common case (single-digit ms instead of a flat 100) and does not flake under load.

Second, smaller point in the same test: teardown at e2e.rs:180-181 (server_handle.abort() + remove_file) only runs when every preceding assertion passed. Any panic leaves the socket behind in /tmp, and tmp_socket_path() (e2e.rs:59-66) names it from `subsec_nanos()` alone — no pid, no process-unique component — so accumulated leftovers are a live collision surface for repeat runs, not just clutter. Deriving the name from std::process::id() as well makes each run's file unambiguous.","status":"open","priority":3,"issue_type":"task","owner":"mark@reviewcommit.com","created_at":"2026-09-13T20:59:14.984596374Z","created_by":"agent","updated_at":"2026-09-13T20:59:14.984596374Z","labels":["elegance","review"],"dependencies":[{"issue_id":"persona-5s4b.61","depends_on_id":"persona-5s4b","type":"parent-child","created_at":"2026-09-13T20:59:15.004811304Z","created_by":"agent","metadata":"{}"}]}

