    // Give the server a moment to bind.
    sleep(Duration::from_millis(100)).await;

What goes wrong: the test's correctness depends on an unwritten timing assumption — that serve() finishes create_dir_all + bind + set_permissions (hire-grpc/src/server.rs:18-33) within 100ms. On a loaded CI runner or under a debug-build cold start that is not guaranteed, and the failure surfaces as 'client connect failed' at e2e.rs:113, which points at the client rather than at the race. There is no retry, so it is a straight flake.

The readiness condition is observable rather than guessable: the socket file's existence is exactly what the client needs. Rewrite as a bounded poll:

    for _ in 0..100 {
        if std::path::Path::new(&socket_path).exists() { break; }
        sleep(Duration::from_millis(10)).await;
    }

That is faster in the common case (single-digit ms instead of a flat 100) and does not flake under load.

